PASSWD(1)                   User Commands

NAME
    passwd - change the password or the full name of an account

SYNOPSIS
    passwd [name]
    passwd -d name
    passwd -n full-name [name]

DESCRIPTION
    passwd sets a new password for the account of the caller, or for the
    account name. passwd is set user id root. Only root may name another
    account. A caller other than root first gives the current password,
    unless the account has none. The new password is asked twice and may
    not be empty. On a terminal the passwords are typed without echo,
    otherwise they are read one per line from standard input, the current
    one first when it is asked for.

    -d removes the password of an account, which then logs in without
    one. Only root may do so. -n sets the full name of the account, the
    fifth field of /etc/passwd, for the caller's own account or, for
    root, any account.

    The password is stored as a SHA-256 crypt hash with a random salt in
    /etc/shadow.

FILES
    /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow

SEE ALSO
    login(1), su(1), useradd(1), passwd(5), index(7)
